module gunbc.design_ruling

import std.types { String }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref, declaration_ref_display_key }
import std.markdown { MarkdownDocument, MarkdownBlock, HeadingBlock, TextInline, H1 }
import gunbc.markdown_helpers { h2, p, hr }
import extdeps.languages.markdown { serialize_markdown_source }

// THE DATED OPERATOR RULINGS DESIGN.md STATES, HELD AS ROWS RATHER THAN NARRATED INLINE.
//
// gunbc.design_document carries reasoning; a ruling is a HISTORY fact -- when, and on whose words --
// plus one live reference: `governs`, the design claim (a gunbc.design_argument row) or document part
// the ruling governs. It is a resolved DeclarationRef rather than a section number, so reordering the
// document moves no ruling, and a deleted or renamed claim fails to resolve.
// The RULE has exactly one home, the document: a row carries no copy of it, so the two cannot drift.
// A row's `reference` is its own declaration as a DeclarationRef; the document cites a ruling by
// passing the row (an import, so a renamed or deleted row fails to resolve), and
// gunbc.documentary_refs takes its ruling references from design_rulings(), so that one roster is
// the population the citation resolver, the render witness and the view below all read.
//
// One row per ruling, each its own declaration, so a citation names one ruling and two lanes adding
// two rulings touch two declarations. The on-demand view is
// `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/design_ruling.dag --function expected_design_rulings_md`;
// it is not committed, for the same contention reason as the failure-mode ledger.

type DesignRuling {
  reference: DeclarationRef
  decided: String
  governs: DeclarationRef
  provenance: String
}

data remote_build_ssh_dispatch_is_an_admitted_scaffold: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "remote_build_ssh_dispatch_is_an_admitted_scaffold"),
  decided: "2026-11-14",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "fail_closed"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-14, direct to session clever-raven-401. Asked whether something could dispatch remote builds sooner than the work-execution-redesign program (roadmap work-execution-redesign, members work-exec-0 to work-exec-6), the operator said, verbatim: \"is there anything we can do to get something sooner? like a simpler approach? i feel like this project shouldn't be super hard - i don't think we need automatic job sizing\". Offered a route that runs the existing provider (gunbc.compute.work_provider_local compute_provide) on another host over one SSH call per request, named as a scaffold whose transport and host order dissolve when work-exec-2-durable-route-first-consumer lands, the operator answered, verbatim: \"yeah this sounds good - i think scaffolding is fine in this case.\" On hosts, verbatim: \"we should use any available fabric host - either we hardcode a reservation or we try to get something else simple working beside the github stuff we already do.\" and \"i think we can add whatever you need to srv3 and srv4 - we also have butch/bobo which should be added\". This is the operator approval outside the diff that a temporary deviation requires. What it admits: the request contract, roster and host order of gunbc.compute.remote_build, a dedicated forced-command SSH key outside the fleet roster, and a build-pool reservation on each joining host carved from its CI runner width (one runner slot per host on srv3 and srv4 is this session's proposal, stated to the operator and not objected to). Dissolution: the SSH transport and the roster order are deleted when work-exec-2's durable route serves the same requests; nothing here amends that program."
}

data one_source_tree_and_the_v2_namespace_dissolves: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "one_source_tree_and_the_v2_namespace_dissolves"),
  decided: "2026-11-22",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-22, direct to session gentle-stag-778, after a caller (the hosted shadow gate's remote steps) failed for omitting one of the two source roots every gunbc run passes. Asked whether the two roots could be consolidated, the operator said, verbatim: \"i didn't intend for two separate source roots - is it okay to sconsolidate those?\". Shown that the split is gunbc.source_layout_rulings R5 (root(v2) = src/v2, every other namespace under dag/), verbatim: \"yeah i think i wanted to rule something about consolidating that down\". Then, verbatim: \"yes, i want to merge these into one tree - i think the answer i want is dag, i didn't intent for things to sprawl this much (basically a duplicate authority issue)\". Shown that the trees import each other both ways and that v2.std and v2.extdeps declare second versions of std and extdeps concepts (Int among them), verbatim: \"yeah i think eventually i want to get rid of the v2 concept - basically i don't quite understand how the trees diverge and want to consolidate/fix it as reasonably as we could\". Asked to confirm that the one-tree move goes first on its own and that src/v1's .dag files stay out of scope, verbatim: \"yeah those are fine, i would just plan/design everything now\". On source roots, verbatim: \"i'd liek to see if we can figure out how to solve source root somehow - i'm trying to get away from filepaths/filesystem stuff for users\". RULED, in those words: (a) gunbc's .dag source is one tree, dag/; src/v2 is merged into it, amending gunbc.source_layout_rulings R5, whose root(v2) = src/v2 clause stands only until the cut lands; (b) the v2 namespace is dissolved, each v2.* family reconciled into the home of the concept it duplicates or renamed where it duplicates none, because the two trees are a duplicate authority; (c) the one-tree move comes first, and src/v1's .dag files are out of scope. DIRECTION, not yet a mechanism: users stop naming source by file path or source root. INTERPRETATION (mine, not the operator's words): (b) applies gunbc.design_argument::single_authority to the namespace and does not license a rename that merges two different concepts; when the cut lands, gunbc.design_ruling::cross_tree_admission_is_namespace_membership has nothing left to decide and is superseded; the mechanism for the direction is designed, not ruled, in gunbc.plans.one_source_tree section 4, under gunbc.design_ruling::source_is_ingested_into_one_universe_and_files_are_discarded. LATER THE SAME DAY, on the plan's open questions. On the existing rulings, verbatim: \"yeah those rulings you mentioned sound like what i rememberd - i didn't mean to fork that, we can make sure to integrate with any existing rulings\". On composed repositories, verbatim: \"to be honest i would do it like bazel where this is a root/workspace/manifest that serves as an entrypoint, my goal is to get away from flags -> actual .dag modeling to represent that stuff as quickly as possible\". On whether removing the seed CLI's root flags is a v1 change the cutover mechanically requires, verbatim: \"it does but i'd approve it anyway\". On the test harness, verbatim: \"we should work through all the consumers and solve this confidently for them\". RULED, in those words: (d) this ruling integrates with the rulings it touches and forks none: it amends gunbc.source_layout_rulings R5 and, at the cut, supersedes gunbc.design_ruling::cross_tree_admission_is_namespace_membership; it extends gunbc.design_ruling::source_is_ingested_into_one_universe_and_files_are_discarded (the manifest declares the bundle that ruling ingests) and gunbc.design_ruling::instrument_is_a_row_not_a_flag (from instruments to every source-naming flag); (e) a workspace is declared by a root manifest, a .dag declaration that is the entry point for everything the checkout holds, as Bazel's workspace root and module file are, and composed repositories are workspaces the manifest names; what flags say today is modeled in .dag instead, as soon as possible; (f) removing --source-root and the entry-file flag from the seed CLI is admitted under gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing::v1_seed_standing, by the operator's approval; (g) every consumer of source roots, the test harness first, is worked through by name and given a stated answer before the flags go. INTERPRETATION (mine): the manifest replaces the .git-ancestor walk and GUNBC_WORKSPACE_ROOT recorded as a scaffold in gunbc.cli_run_workspace_root_scaffold, and gunbc.source_layout becomes the content that manifest declares rather than a second authority beside it; the consumer roster is in gunbc.plans.one_source_tree.",
}

data hosted_ci_bridges_to_remote_builds_and_adopts_work_execution_redesign: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "hosted_ci_bridges_to_remote_builds_and_adopts_work_execution_redesign"),
  decided: "2026-11-19",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "replacement_migration"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-19, to session clever-raven-401, deciding the CI substrate while the work-execution-redesign program (roadmap work-execution-redesign, members work-exec-0 to work-exec-6) is built out. On static sizing, verbatim: \"i don't think we need static sizing anymore - i think we should just size the popular workloads as we know them and then reserve the worst case dynamically in the ledgers right? i think we are negotiating between a few dying architectures\". On the CI shape, verbatim: \"OK i think basically we should start using standard 2 core github runners just as placeholder runners to shell out to remote builds - and then we need a better flexible build system\". The program, a proposal of 2026-11-08, was put to the operator as adopt-or-not, with today's remote-build path as a temporary bridge; the answer, verbatim: \"yeah please\". Asked when to run the placement-backend spike (roadmap work-exec-1-placement-backend-spike), verbatim: \"after is fine\", meaning after the fast path works. DECIDED. (a) work-execution-redesign is ADOPTED: it is no longer a proposal for operator discussion but the accepted plan for the flexible build system, and its members stand in the order the program already orders them. (b) The interim bridge is ADMITTED: CI runs on standard GitHub-hosted runners for public repos and on small self-hosted placeholder runners for private repos, and both drive the remote-build client over the tailnet as an ephemeral node joining via Tailscale OAuth. On the tag: the operator first declined to fork a second tag, verbatim: \"i don't want to keep forking tags now, its too early\"; later the same day the live tailnet policy showed that a tag:ci runner could not reach a tag:ci:22 host, so the operator approved a dedicated dispatch tag, verbatim: \"OK for ci-dispatch, as long as its all modeled/recorded for tailscale convergence\". The runners therefore join as tag:ci-dispatch; that tag's ACL grant and credential are modeled and recorded for tailscale convergence in the fleet-auth work carried by PR 16752, which cites this row rather than adding a second ruling. This sits together with the srv3/srv4 six-slot carve. This is the admitted scaffold of gunbc.design_ruling::remote_build_ssh_dispatch_is_an_admitted_scaffold with its admitted scope EXTENDED, not its status upgraded: the remote build now carries the CI substrate for the required check for the bridge's duration, but it stays an admitted scaffold and dissolves on that ruling's own condition -- the SSH transport and roster order are deleted when work-exec-2's durable route and work-exec-5's verdict-from-receipt serve the same requests. The bridge is declared as roadmap node remote-build-ci-bridge, an ordered member of the work-execution-redesign project; its dissolution into work-exec-2's route and work-exec-5's receipt is carried by its node text and this ruling, not by a prerequisite edge -- dissolution is not a dependency, so no edge joins the bridge to its successors. (c) Static slot sizing is FROZEN: no new investment beyond the carve, consistent with gunbc.design_argument::replacement_migration -- the fixed self-hosted runner width and per-slot MemoryMax and the carved build-pool seat keep serving the required check until cutover and are not extended. (d) The hosted shadow job is ADMITTED against gunbc.design_ruling::job_roster_closed_to_growth as a transition measure that dissolves at cutover; work-exec-5 already records the shadow roster addition as approved (roadmap work-exec-5-ci-verdict-from-receipt: \"first the optional shadow job (roster addition already approved)\"), which this ruling re-confirms rather than newly granting."
}

data butch_serving_retirement_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "butch_serving_retirement_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21"),
  decided: "2026-11-21",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "selection_precedes_convergence"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-21, to session clever-raven-401. Asked about retiring the serving engines on butch (srv14) so its memory goes to remote builds, the operator said, verbatim: \"yes, you can retire the butch stuff, or actually i'll do that separatley, you can record it now\". In the same message the operator set the priority, verbatim: \"i need to get maximum remote build bandwidth asap, because we're fiarly behind\". DECIDED: the operator retires butch's serving engines personally, outside this repository's converge route, so the fleet-converge host_unit_retirement mode proposed in PR 16831 is not the route for this retirement. INTERPRETATION (mine, not the operator's words): this ruling records who performs the retirement, not that it has happened; butch's admission as a remote-build host still waits on a read of the host showing the serving units gone and its memory envelope measured (fleet-converge host_memory_observe), and on the roster and capacity rows that read grounds. Until that read, nothing in the model treats butch's serving reservation as released.",
}

data butch_tailnet_join_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "butch_tailnet_join_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21"),
  decided: "2026-11-21",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "selection_precedes_convergence"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-21, to session clever-raven-401, later the same day as gunbc.design_ruling::butch_serving_retirement_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21. Asked whether butch's (srv14) tailnet join is also operator-handled or waits on the parked modelled join route (PR 16820, held behind the fleet-converge budget question), the operator said, verbatim: \"I will do the tailnet join + engine retirement now\", and then, verbatim: \"but you'll need to record/model that in the repo\". DECIDED: the operator joins butch to the tailnet by hand, so PR 16820's join route is not the route for butch, and the repository must model the result. INTERPRETATION (mine, not the operator's words): the obligations are that butch's tailnet identity becomes its fleet anchor and its desired state stops declaring the serving engines the operator retired, so a later converge cannot bring them back; both are grounded in reads of the host, never in this row, and this row records who performs the join, not that it has happened.",
}

data thermal_runner_cap_lifted_2026_11_08: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "thermal_runner_cap_lifted_2026_11_08"),
  decided: "2026-11-08",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "selection_precedes_convergence"),
  provenance: "Operator, to bold-wren-111 on 2026-11-08, verbatim: \"could you please help me bring up the stopped urnners on srv3/4, and srv1 - i think we have a lot of stranded capacity right now.\" then \"we had heat concerns but this outweighs them\" and \"we'll also need to record that in convergence/allocation int he repo\". ENDS the 2026-11-05 incident override that held srv3 and srv4 at 12 runners per box (gunbc.plans.host_thermal_operating_point B0 and section 10 decision 1, which recommended keeping 12 until P5). The override was never a model row -- it lived only as hand drop-ins on the hosts -- so no allocation literal changes: the committed width stays the one gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation gunbc_runner_committed_width derives (18 on srv3 and srv4 at this revision), and the operator then directed an EMERGENCY HAND RESTORE (\"can we not run this by hand? its a bit of an emergency - you can get to srv3/srv4 thru srv1\"), overriding for this one action the 2026-08-07 ruling that host mutation goes through the apply workflow; the queued converge run 42534959803 was cancelled because its bracket would have drained the restored slots. Hand actions, 2026-11-08 ~23:30 UTC, mirroring runner_slot_actuation slot_activation_plan (unmask, enable, start) over the committed population 1..18 minus the fabric slots 6 and 7: srv4 unmask --runtime 01-05 and 08-14, enable and start 01-05 and 08-18; srv3 enable and start 15-18 and remove the hand drop-in 95-overnight-cap.conf (Restart=no) from 15-18. srv4 15-18 kept that drop-in until the operator answered (\"i'm not concerned about memory errors on these machines\"), after which it was removed from srv4 15-18 the same way; 19-21 on both hosts are outside the committed width and untouched. A later runner_host_file_converge apply is the route that reconciles the hosts to the model. srv4 CARRIES A SECOND, SEPARATE DRAIN this ruling does not lift by itself: 96-ecc-drain-note.conf on srv4 01-05 and 08-14 records a 2026-11-07 ECC drain (decision cool-badger-99 msg_ada8ac0b): a hard reset after 18,717 corrected DRAM ECC errors in one boot and a prior fatal hardware error on 10-30, undo after an operator DIMM/SEL check. Read at restore: EDAC mc0 ce_count 181 and ue_count 0 about 25 h into the current boot. The operator then judged corrected memory errors normal for these boards and kept srv4 in admission; the 96-ecc-drain-note.conf files remain as stale comments with no directive. srv1 needed no hand action: its actions-runner population (srv1-01 transitional, srv1-02 deploy) already equals the model, and its microVM slots 13 and 14 are a controller bring-up, not a unit start. Observed before the restore (runner names on in-progress jobs and systemctl, 2026-11-08 ~23:20 UTC): srv3 ran 12 slots, srv1 ran srv1-01 and srv1-02, srv4 ran none. STATED TENSION, NOT A WAIVER OF ANYTHING ELSE: the 2026-11-05 decision that DIMMs stay at or below 85 C is not restated or withdrawn by this ruling; the operator judged the throughput loss to outweigh the heat risk at full width, and the lasting operating point still comes from the plan's P5 selection."
}

data printer_start_gate_is_the_approval_not_the_ref: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "printer_start_gate_is_the_approval_not_the_ref"),
  decided: "2026-11-05",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "selection_precedes_convergence"),
  provenance: "Operator, to sleek-wren-619 on 2026-11-05, verbatim: \"what is reviewed main - its not that sensitive - we should be able to print anything from anywhere with an approval from the app\". SUPERSEDES the 2026-10-25 design-A ruling that only reviewed main's code may start a printer (gunbc.fleet.print_converge print_pass_ref, print_pass_route and PrinterStartRefusedOffReviewedMain, deleted; gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow the workflow route's printer WIF sign-in stays main-only, because the printer-lan provider's attribute condition pins ref to main (gunbc.auth.oidc_claim_pins fleet_converge_workflow_claim_pins) and that is unchanged; the off-main route is the shell route, which reads host custody and holds no Google identity). The gate is the approval, which binds printer, project sha256, credential version, attempt, the selected AMS slot and the printer state the human saw (gunbc.fleet.printer_actuation print_subject); the ref is not a gate.",
}

data printer_credential_is_held_in_host_custody: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "printer_credential_is_held_in_host_custody"),
  decided: "2026-11-05",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "consumption"),
  provenance: "Operator, to sleek-wren-619 on 2026-11-05, on where the printer LAN access code lives: \"i don't really know what the precedent is - i'll let you decide\". Decided by sleek-wren-619 and confirmed to snappy-ibex-860: the code is held in HOST CUSTODY on srv1 (gunbc.fleet.host_credential_custody_converge, rows Printer01AccessCode and Printer02AccessCode), root:gunbc-fleet 0640 so only the print runner principal reads it, copied once from its Secret Manager locus (gunbc.fleet.printer_federation) by a run that has WIF. The custody record is JSON \{code, sm_resolved\} so the approval subject keeps binding the credential version. The shell route (gunbc.fleet.printer_shell_run) reads custody, never Secret Manager, via gunbc.fleet.printer_credential_source PrinterCredentialSource = SecretManager | HostCustody; a custody read is not an AccessTokenSource arm because it is not a token.",
}

data machine_width_composition_has_an_intersection_carrier: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "machine_width_composition_has_an_intersection_carrier"),
  decided: "2026-11-01",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "closed_grounded_substrate"),
  provenance: "Operator CARRIER ruling relayed by quick-newt-573 to bold-dove-340, message msg_0cf79c9c-cc1f-4002-a6ed-79dae1ec7547; operator intersection intent relayed in msg_dcf2da48-1255-435d-b210-690732972a9f. Compose<A,C> denotes intersection: for Int/UInt and MachineWidth<N>, the carrier is the algebra value restricted to the machine interval, with the width retained in its type. This is NOT Compose<A,C> = A, the transparent alias rejected by the arithmetic ledger. Real crossings motivating the ruling are git.object_store, MAC qualified_octet_members, openssl, OIDC DER and roadmap auth hex. CORRECTION to the escalation: v2.std.node_minimal SpanRef.file_id was cited as a core-node example but is a declared scaffold outside the self-host and v2-native-cli import closures; it is not executed evidence. The ruling supersedes std.integer's no-inhabitant prescription for this integer-width intersection only, not arbitrary Compose applications. Model first; emitter changes follow only after model review. No current runtime or emitted-width guarantee is claimed by recording this ruling.",
}

data printer_execution_hold_deleted: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "printer_execution_hold_deleted"),
  decided: "2026-11-09",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "consumption"),
  provenance: "Operator, direct to sleek-wren-619 on 2026-11-09, after the stale-hold release refused with permission_denied on the root-owned printer-starts parent: \"we need to make the prints idempotent - i would just delete the concept of the printer hold, it was well intended but not designed/implemented correctly, and clean up all of that state - its supposed to protect against duplicate prints on the same printer, but i'll just do that manually for now until it becomes an issue\". The printer execution hold (gunbc.fleet.printer_execution_hold), its operator-observation release route (the hold-release-request printer operation) and the flat-layout cutover step that depended on it (gunbc.fleet.printer_state_layout printer_state_move) are deleted, and the flat entries left in /var/lib/gunbc/printer-starts are removed by hand. A print start stays idempotent per attempt through the create-once start claim (gunbc.fleet.printer_actuation printer_start_claim_commit, ExpectSlotAbsent) and stays gated by the bed-clear approval; exclusion of two concurrent controllers on one printer is held by the operator by hand until it becomes an issue. Supersedes the release route named in printer_start_hold_lifted_2026_11_04.",
}

data printer_start_hold_lifted_2026_11_04: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "printer_start_hold_lifted_2026_11_04"),
  decided: "2026-11-04",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "how_to_work"),
  provenance: "Operator, relayed by wise-tern-646 to witty-tern-466 on 2026-11-04: the 2026-10-21 standing hold on printer starts (do not touch /var/lib/gunbc/printer-starts) is LIFTED. It is released through the modeled route, the fleet-converge printer mode's hold-release-request operation (gunbc.fleet.printer_execution_hold printer_hold_release_request), which files the operator card and releases nothing by hand. The same day's two PETG prints were started out of band (a hand-run prepare_request_plate and a hand-written MQTT publish after a pasted token); that path is not the modeled route and is superseded by print_converge, whose start now takes its AMS tray from gunbc.fleet.printer_ams_loading select_ams_tray.",
}

data fleet_authorized_keys_on_every_install_route: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "fleet_authorized_keys_on_every_install_route"),
  decided: "2026-11-07",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "Operator ruling 2026-11-07, direct to merry-fox-82: \"the fleet public key may be placed in authorized_keys on mtcollins1, BUT generically for ALL installed machines, not as an mtcollins1 special case\". The fleet roster is admitted once, in gunbc.fleet.fleet_ssh_access fleet_authorized_keys (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority), and every install/boot route that brings up an OS the fleet reaches consumes that ONE roster -- never a second key list and never a machine-named row (the one rule reaching every sibling, gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization). Routes that carry it: the PXE autoinstall route and gunbc.host.host_authorized_keys_reconcile; the boot-artifact-delivery, seeded-install-media, and extdeps.os.ubuntu_autoinstall user-data routes, which produce the gunbc.os_install UbuntuAutoinstallPayload documents whose autoinstall ssh section the installer writes to the INSTALLED target. One gap was closed by this ruling: the Always Free OCI launch route, whose oci_free_authorized_keys hand-joined the two key constants (gunbc.oracle_oci.instance_ensure), re-deciding the roster route-by-route and now mapping over fleet_authorized_keys. The mtcollins1 BMC virtual-media boot is NOT covered by this ruling's implementation: mtcollins1_boot_run selects runner_host_boot_medium (gunbc.runner.runner_host_medium), a diskless live session that falls through to Subiquity's interactive sections rather than completing an install and starts no sshd; the autoinstall ssh section is a target-installer feature (subiquity @508bcc48 SSHController.load_autoinstall_data -> configure_cloud_init writes under /target during postinstall), so a roster placed there never reaches the running session and a rendered autoinstall ssh section is NOT evidence of live authorization. The live-runner follow-up this ruling named is now CLOSED by the runner_host_medium boot stage (this PR, gunbc.runner.runner_host_medium runner_host_fleet_authorized_keys_command): it seeds the live session's own authorized_keys from fleet_authorized_keys and hardens and reads back sshd, witnessed over the rendered live stage (not a nested-only ssh config), so this route now carries the roster too. SCOPE -- routes that are NOT the fleet-admin authorized_keys channel, stated here and deliberately unchanged: workspace microVMs write the request owner's key (workload access, not fleet admin); runner self-enrollment and Spark bootstrap select only fleet_automation_ssh_key for specific service accounts on an EXISTING OS, which is not an install route; the machine-intake census medium (gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_census_image) boots to a hardware census and halts, establishing no admin login, so its live seed carries no ssh posture; and public NoCloud and ubuntu_autoinstall_seed admission validate syntax only -- roster coverage is the caller's, and each caller that boots an administered machine supplies it from fleet_authorized_keys. No route may name a key the model does not admit.",
}

data bmc_secure_goes_live_per_controller_family: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "bmc_secure_goes_live_per_controller_family"),
  decided: "2026-10-28",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "Operator answers to session cool-owl-599 on staffing the brief that retires gunbc.tools.bmc_onboard for BMC credential rotation. (1) The state-shaped BmcSecure (gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_secure cut O1b) may become the live authority for the ASRock ALTRAD8UD / OpenBMC family while the rotation-event path stays frozen as mtcollins1's standing under ruling C (eager-gull-22, 2026-10-04), on the condition that mtcollins1's own cutover is left near a flag switch rather than stranded: \"yeah thats fine assuming the code is basically a flag switch to finish the mtcollins migration - i don't want to leave it on an island/stranded\". (2) A build that forces the published credential's change on first login is behaviour modeled per version and interacted with, and in that state the password write is the first rotation; the converge rotates at the earliest point and never redundantly: \"thats behavior we have to model per version and interact with ... Our workflow should be smart enough to rotate credentials at the earliest place, and not do it redundantly\" (gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_credential_policy). (3) Failed-login probes against an account with a lockout budget are avoided where a derivation is available, and otherwise accounted for: \"we can't do lockout bound reads basically - or, we have to account for them - i would avoid it if possible\" (bmc_secure PublishedCredentialRetiredOnTheManagedAccount). (4) srv1-srv4, the same board family, are in scope for the cutover; the Supermicro H11SSL units are folded in, with their per-unit bootstrap credential held in the operator's sheet for now.",
}

data one_serving_context_cap: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "one_serving_context_cap"),
  decided: "2026-10-30",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "Operator, relayed by calm-moth-166 to calm-crab-645 on 2026-10-30 (ruling D3 on gunbc#15439, then at about 20:06Z \"get these all on the same page, 229k is fine\"): the serving pool holds ONE context cap of 229,376 tokens. The Strata engines on h11 and srv3 already launch with it, the router advertises it for srv1-vllm and ctrl-vast-pool, and srv1's vLLM launch moves from lil-serve's profile default of 262,144 to it. The value is held once, at gunbc.serving.serving_context_cap serving_fleet_context_cap; srv1 is not relaunched for it before the operator's srv1 DIMM decision.",
}

data srv3_experts_mmap_under_service_cap: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv3_experts_mmap_under_service_cap"),
  decided: "2026-11-09",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_layout", decl_name: "srv3_routed_expert_artifact"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-09, verbatim 'sounds good' to bold-wren-111's proposal to switch srv3 to --mmap-experts under a capped memory limit like srv4. bold-wren-111 executed it by hand on srv3 ~22:10Z: /home/ubuntu/strata-pool/cfg.json gained --mmap-experts after '--expert-cache auto' (backup cfg.json.bak-20261109-pre-mmap); new drop-in /etc/systemd/system/strata-pool.service.d/60-expert-l1-cap.conf with MemoryHigh=96G, MemoryMax=112G; strata-pool restarted once. After: the engine log reads 'experts via mmap (--mmap-experts; the GGUF shards in place, no experts.bin)'; service MemoryCurrent ~79-81 GiB (was ~116 GiB with the 46.84 GiB expert arena pinned); memory.stat anon ~8.7 GB and file ~72.5 GB; after a first cold request that read ~58 GB, warm requests read only KBs to tens of MB of OS storage. The conversation cache is unchanged at 65536 MiB in 24 slots at --batch 8. This records a hand action: the serving model now reads srv3's expert tier as srv4's evictable shape under the service cap (the cap the RAM L1 bound), not the pinned 46.84 GiB private arena under the 110 GiB slice the model held before.",
}

