  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator ruling cutting three of seven jobs. On 2026-09-15 the same reasoning cut the push-on-main trigger: the merge queue's required run already proves the landing sha.",
}

data ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth"),
  decided: "2026-11-07",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, directly in session nimble-hawk-457 on 2026-11-07, about 01:45-01:55Z, in reply to v2-bootstrap being cancelled at its 90-minute budget in the parse-population walk and #16006 proposing 90 -> 150 minutes (escalation msg_ebf6e9b0, approved by default with no human reply). The operator's words, complete: 'i do not want that timeout increased', 'please do not increase it', then 'we also have to ban any increases without explicit signoff/approval from me'. RULED, in those words: no increase without the operator's explicit sign-off or approval. SECOND OPERATOR ANSWER, 2026-11-07, verbatim operator words, on whether the ban covers memory budgets: 'we are supposed to be lower CI memory budgets, not raising them'. RULED from that answer: CI memory budgets (the memory envelope of a job, step or runner slot) are covered by the same ban, closed to growth without the operator's explicit sign-off or approval, and the operator's stated direction for them is to LOWER them, not raise them. INTERPRETATION, not the operator's words: keen-otter-392's operational interpretation (msg_42c3c192) of the time-budget scope, held as the conservative interim scope until the operator confirms or narrows it (memory budgets are settled by the operator's second answer above, not by this interpretation): (1) it covers any raise of a time budget above its previously approved value: a CI or fleet workflow job or step timeout, the authority row it derives from (for example gunbc.ci.repository_ci_profile roster.timeout), or an individual workload booking such as the srv1 launcher RUNTIME_S; (2) a capability, such as a 7200-second maximum, is not an authorization, and a proposal never granted is not an approved ceiling; (3) relabelling, splitting a budget into phases, aggregating jobs or adding retries to exceed the approved total is an increase; (4) existing exact grants are unchanged. AUTHOR'S INFERENCES, also not the operator's words: a default-decided escalation is not sign-off; lowering a budget is not an increase, but it is no blanket permission for edits, pushes or runs, the lowered value becomes the approved baseline, and raising it back needs explicit sign-off; this ruling itself grants no push, publication or landing. Runtime reduction is the selected remediation direction for an overrun; that does not establish that every overrun is a code defect. REQUIREMENT: a raise lands only with a ruling row recording the operator's explicit approval of that raise. ENFORCEMENT: by review only; budgets are literals in gunbc.ci.repository_ci_profile and no gate reads them. NEXT RUNG: a budget field that carries the ruling admitting its value, so that a raise with no ruling row citing the operator's sign-off does not construct. DesignRuling has no typed field for a requirement or a next rung, so both are stated here; adding fields would reshape every ruling row. The requirement is therefore prose, not typed. The #16006 raise was withdrawn and held on these words.",
}

data srv15_versions_read_backstop_admitted: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv15_versions_read_backstop_admitted"),
  decided: "2026-11-19",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator chat to smart-crane-730, 2026-11-19T02:35Z (typed directly in the Claude Code chat of smart-crane-730; no dashboard message id). QUESTION as the manager put it: '#16640: raise the gcp-iam-bootstrap-request job's time limit from 284 to 299 minutes?' Context given: the request step stays at its derived 259, and the +15 covers exactly the three 5-minute srv15 versions-read steps moving into that job. OPERATOR'S WORDS, verbatim: '1. OK'. RULED, in those words: this raise -- the gcp-iam-bootstrap-request job backstop 284 -> 299 in fleet-converge, with the request step kept at its derived 259 (approval_poll_step_timeout_minutes over the converge file window; no undocumented subtraction) and the +15 being the three 5-minute modeled steps (mint, read, upload of the srv15 BMC secret-versions receipt, each at gunbc_ci_aux_step_timeout_minutes) moved into that job ahead of the approval gate, the fix for the deadlock of run 44134741714 -- is admitted. The admission covers exactly this job, this delta and this reason; it is not a general budget release.",
}

// An operator-approved CI time-budget raise of ONE fold, kept as its own row (design_ruling is the
// event log). This is the row that satisfies gunbc.design_ruling::ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth's
// REQUIREMENT -- a raise lands only with a ruling row recording the operator's explicit approval.
data srv1_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_raised: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv1_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_raised"),
  decided: "2026-11-21",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-21, on escalation msg_0a75dc85-1070-4799-98cc-65ac30c4deb4, answered by the operator in msg_35ef1b77-8cbb-445b-8e26-8bda69f0ac05 around 21:5xZ -- AN EXPLICIT OPERATOR ANSWER, NOT A TIMEOUT DEFAULT. QUESTION, verbatim: \"srv1 deploy now passes step 74 but hits the 30-min step timeout (21-min compile + per-file source installs). Raise the dashboard-deploy step timeout to 60 min?\" OPERATOR'S WORDS, verbatim: \"A: yes, raise dashboard-deploy step timeout 30->60 min, then redeploy\". RULED, in those words: this raise -- the dashboard deploy fold (gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow::fleet_converge_dashboard_deploy_step, the \"Deploy dashboard to srv1\" step) moving off the shared 30-minute live-deploy tier (gunbc.fleet.fleet_workflow_steps::gunbc_ci_live_deploy_step_timeout_minutes) onto its own 60-minute budget (gunbc.fleet.fleet_workflow_steps::gunbc_ci_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_minutes) -- is admitted as a CI time-budget increase under gunbc.design_ruling::ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth, which closes CI time and memory budgets to growth without the operator's explicit sign-off. The admission covers exactly this step, this value and this reason; it is not a general budget release. WHY THE JOB MOVED TOO, part of the SAME approval: the dashboard-deploy job's backstop (gunbc.fleet.fleet_workflow_steps::gunbc_ci_dashboard_deploy_job_backstop_timeout_minutes) is derived to cover every fold in that job, so it must admit the 60-minute step; its bound rises 314 -> 344 and is recorded here rather than granted silently. The frame was split so that no OTHER job moves: the instance-scope deploy, the microVM controller install and its tenancy admission, and the approval-ntfy retire keep the shared 30-minute tier and their job bounds are UNCHANGED; no job is added, and no other timeout is touched. WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, named so it is not mistaken for done: run 44947639071 (job 136980921499) was killed by the 30-minute ceiling after its compile.reconcile ran 18 minutes (resolve_entry_graph_inclusive 1279122 ms over a 1909-file closure) and the fold then spent about 9 minutes installing the source trees one RunArgv per file. THOSE TWO ARE OPEN ROOT-CAUSE FOLLOW-UPS, NOT DONE here: the 18-minute compile reconcile and the per-file source installs. The raise buys wall-clock so srv1's gunbc-roadmap can deploy; it funds an EXISTING cost (the same 18-minute reconcile had already shown in the 2026-11-20 run), it does not make the fold faster, and a slower fold is what the next raise would be asked for unless the two follow-ups are taken.",
}

