}

data run_realizes_only_demanded_declarations: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "run_realizes_only_demanded_declarations"),
  decided: "2026-11-10",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.rung_drop.gunbc_run_unreached_declarations_not_realized", decl_name: "gunbc_run_unreached_declarations_not_realized"),
  provenance: "Operator ruling, escalation msg_006569ab, decided 2026-11-10, explicit -- NOT a timeout default -- recorded via session cool-badger-99. Question as put to the operator, verbatim: \"Rule on #16069/#16156: allow gunbc run to typecheck/realize only what its entry reaches, with the demanded_scope controls run by hand until a follow-up enrolls them?\" Operator words, verbatim: \"Yes, both as described\". The operator's words end here; the same ruling is recorded for the guarantee-ladder claim in gunbc.design_ruling::gunbc_run_checks_only_what_its_entry_reaches (PR #16069), which this row does not restate -- it governs only this lane's rung drop. Interpretation, this session's words and not the operator's: this authorizes gunbc run to realize only the non-function declarations a demanded declaration names transitively, where the population is exactly the declarations excluded by the declared read inventories (v1.compiler.infer.realization_row_names: the spelled extent plus the reader families realization_row_edges and realization_provider_row_edges, closed over the typed side by the bounded fixpoint), every edge outside the realized set refuses as the blocking located Diagnostic.DeclarationRealizationGap, and the silent-undeclared-read residue is claimed by NOTHING -- it sits below the guarantee ladder (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder), is not authorized by the drop row, and has its own next-rung restoration trigger (instrument census consults to refuse whenever a census-resolvable name is not realized). The whole-closure typecheck (compile, emit, self-verify, every EveryDeclaration witness) is untouched; this is a gunbc run lowering alone, reversible with the scope. The demanded_scope controls run by hand under this ruling are enrolled by follow-up node adhoc-0eedbb6e-a35.",
}

data namespace_xl_migration_program_is_bankrupt: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "namespace_xl_migration_program_is_bankrupt"),
  decided: "2026-11-10",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "how_to_work"),
  provenance: "Operator ruling 2026-11-10, direct to session nimble-newt-61, during the census retirement (gunb-ai/gunbc#16280). A review the operator pasted into the session put the decision, verbatim: \"Retire the obsolete namespace migration program, not the namespace semantics it was supposed to make correct. Preserve the actual compiler behavior: declaring-identity resolution, import and qualified-path binding, cross-tree namespace admission, exact/ambiguous/unbound refusal, and reference conservation. Existing focused regression tests for these behaviors should survive independently of their XL status.\" and \"Proceed as a separate architectural deletion. The November 8 decisions supply the authority. Ensure namespace_cut_stage, compiler_frontend_program_status, interlocks, roadmap nodes and seed-retirement handbacks consistently reflect the new destination. Keep actual namespace behavior and its targeted controls.\" Asked to confirm, the operator answered, verbatim: \"yeah you can bankrupt that\". Extends plans_reset_and_audit_recommendations_2026_11_08 (planning carriers) to the namespace-XL migration machinery itself: the XL stage chain and subject roster, the step-0 candidate/strip/canary and binding-provenance contract, the legacy baseline/binding XL-1 chain, the XL-2 rehearsal, the declaring-identity spelling census and the repair-input roster, and the Namespace* milestones of gunbc.compiler_frontend_program_interlock, with no successor. Carried by gunb-ai/gunbc#16281.",
}

data printer_ci_budget_raised_for_the_four_hour_card: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "printer_ci_budget_raised_for_the_four_hour_card"),
  decided: "2026-11-13",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, via fierce-bee-888 on gunbc#16325, 2026-11-13, words verbatim: \"16325 - did you ask your side chat - and yes you can raise those itmeouts\" [sic]. This is the explicit sign-off ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth requires; not a default-closed escalation. The raise this records: the fleet-converge PRINTER job's timeout 98 -> 319 minutes and its converge step 63 -> 289 minutes, generated from printer_converge_step_timeout_minutes with the card at the 4 h policy row. Arithmetic, every term a declared row: approval_request_window (240 min) + preparation allowance (44 min) + checkout allowance from gunbc_ci_aux_step_timeout_minutes (5 min) = 289; + tail allowance = 319; under max_job_timeout_minutes (360). The card change (printer_operator_approval_window -> approval_request_window) is the operator's own request that approval cards last at least a few hours; the budget is its direct consequence. The interim scope of ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth names fleet workflow job and step timeouts; this row is that recording.",
}

data serve_entry_verdict_step_raised_to_45_minutes: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "serve_entry_verdict_step_raised_to_45_minutes"),
  decided: "2026-11-14",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-14, relayed verbatim by session neat-badger-614 (msg_a27efa77) to session bright-ibex-807. Question as put to the operator: raise the fleet-converge plan run's serve-entry compile verdict step bound from 30 to 45 minutes (gunbc_ci_plan_serve_entry_verdict_step_timeout_minutes, ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth). Operator words, verbatim: \"yes thats fine\". This is the explicit sign-off ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth requires; not a default-closed escalation. The raise this records: that one step, 30 -> 45 minutes. Evidence: plan run 43593733352 was killed at 30m in this step; on srv1 the child compile measured 1607 s and the step 1798 s wall, 13.6 GB peak. The fleet job backstop (gunbc_ci_fleet_job_backstop_timeout_minutes) does not add this term, so no job timeout changes. INTERPRETATION, not the operator's words: the selected remediation direction stays runtime reduction (a faster serve-entry compile), after which this bound comes back down; lowering it needs no sign-off.",
}

// The operator's sign-off for the arrival-converge budget raise, recorded under the rule that reserves
// CI time and memory budgets to them (ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth). The text was staged for review on
// PR #16691 and is granted here; this same change flips the emitter's two call sites, so the permission and
// the figures it authorizes cannot be separated. Reviews 96450, 96488, 96494, 96500, 96505 and 96506 are
// what put it in this order.
data srv15_arrival_converge_step_raised_to_21_minutes: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv15_arrival_converge_step_raised_to_21_minutes"),
  decided: "2026-11-18",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-18T21:13Z (the answer was given in chat at about 21:13:23Z), relayed verbatim by session smart-crane-730 from its own session chat; there is no dashboard message id, so the citation is: operator chat to smart-crane-730, 2026-11-18T21:13Z. Question as put to the operator: \"#16691: raise the srv15 arrival step's time limit from 5 to 21 minutes, and its job's from 25 to 51. srv15 can't be installed until this is applied.\" Operator words, verbatim: \"1. yeah thats fine\". This is the explicit sign-off ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth requires; it is not a default-closed escalation, and lowering these figures later needs no sign-off at all. The figures agreed to: the fleet-converge arrival-converge job's backstop 25 -> 51 minutes and its 'arrival converge under the standing grant and arrival interlock' step 5 -> 21 minutes. Both are generated, not hand-typed: gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow fleet_converge_arrival_converge_step_timeout_minutes builds the step figure as fleet_converge_gunbc_run_step_timeout_minutes over the Secret Manager read bound (gunbc_ci_sm_max_time), the door's IPMI legs (gunbc.machine_intake.arrival_run arrival_wet_ipmi_legs_bound) and the prior-life capture's legs (gunbc.machine_intake.prior_life_capture_live live_prior_life_legs_bound), on the existing resolve allowance (gunbc_ci_gunbc_run_resolve_step_timeout_minutes); fleet_converge_arrival_converge_job_backstop_timeout_minutes builds the job through checked_job_timeout_minutes, and fleet_converge_arrival_converge_backstop_holds refuses emission past max_job_timeout_minutes rather than clamping. Arithmetic, every term a declared row: step = ceiling(60 s + 302 s + 140 s + 59 s = 561 s) = 10 min plus one 5-minute allowance = 660 s = 21 min; job = prelude 5 + artifact transfer 10 + three 5-minute neighbours + 21 = 51, against the 360-minute ceiling. Evidence for the need: arrival run 44249358201 at 418bf0a1674 was killed at 05m00s in that step at 2026-11-18T07:17:51Z with the fold never entered -- [pre-entry] 107 s over 523 closure files, compile.frontend 80 s, process max_rss 2191 MB, the admission step before it green -- and zero ipmitool or curl lines in the step, so srv15's controller was contacted zero times and its lockout budget was untouched. INTERPRETATION, not the operator's words: the remediation direction stays the reduction of the pre-entry fixed cost (roadmap node gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.gunbc_run_pays_a_large_fixed_pre_entry_cost_per_invocation); when that cost comes down, this bound comes down with it.",
}

data mtcollins1_bmc_cold_reset_step_raised_to_67_minutes: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "mtcollins1_bmc_cold_reset_step_raised_to_67_minutes"),
  decided: "2026-11-18",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-18T21:13Z, operator chat to smart-crane-730 (no message id), relayed verbatim by smart-crane-730 (msg_cab7dd83) through merry-fox-82 (msg_a124a26d) to session quiet-gull-101. Question as put to the operator: \"#16668: raise mtcollins1's BMC reset step from 35 to 67 minutes.\" Operator words, verbatim: \"2. yeah thats fine\". This is the explicit sign-off ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth requires; not a default-closed escalation. The raise this records: that one step, the fleet-converge MtCollins1BmcColdReset step bound (gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_bmc_cold_reset mtcollins1_bmc_cold_reset_step_timeout_minutes), 35 -> 67 minutes, carried by gunb-ai/gunbc#16668; its typed grant is gunbc.ci.ci_budget_raise mtcollins1_bmc_cold_reset_step_raised_to_67_minutes_grant. Why: gunbc#16659 -- run 44210890542 accepted a cold reset on one answer after the controller came back, and about 45 min later (run 44217632446) the controller was silent again; acceptance now requires the controller to stay available through a sustained window W on the monotonic clock. Arithmetic, every term a declared row: W = mtcollins1_boot_prelude_allowance (120 s) + mtcollins1_boot_issue_allowance (120 s) + megarac_convergence_allowance (469 s) = 709 s; the window's budget is structural, mtcollins1_bmc_cold_reset_sustain_polls_at_most (ceil(709/15) + 1 = 49) x (poll interval 15 s + read ceiling 25 s) = 1960 s, because the window's own clock cannot bound its wall time (side chat on gunbc#16668, F2); step allowance = prelude 120 + 4 single reads x 25 + 46 loss and recovery polls x 40 + 1960 = 4020 s = 67 min (was 2060 s = 35 min). The mtcollins1-boot job timeout (205 min) is not exceeded. The generated .github/workflows/fleet-converge.yml moves from 35 to 67 through its emitter's regen, in the same PR.",
}

// The srv15-arrival environment's REQUIRED REVIEWER, selected by the operator. The model row that
// consumes it (gunbc.auth.github_deployment_environment.srv15_arrival_reviewer_decision) carries
// this row by reference; the decision lives here, not in a comment or a chat id string.
data srv15_arrival_reviewer_selected_by_operator: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv15_arrival_reviewer_selected_by_operator"),
  decided: "2026-11-15",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "Operator decision on the escalation 'who approves srv15-arrival environment runs (required reviewer principal)?', asked by smart-crane-730 (msg_bda70b7b, recommending the repository owner), answered as relayed verbatim by smart-crane-730 (msg_1d59b8bb): \"Reviewer decided: briansrls (GitHub user id 11205878), operator msg_a6718a1d, approved by default.\" The reviewer is the GitHub user with login briansrls and numeric user id 11205878; the environment read-back must show exactly this principal as the environment's required reviewer. No email address is recorded, per the brief's no-email rule; the principal is cited by user id, which is the immutable GitHub fact.",
}

data no_ci_claim_execution_until_native_frontier_clears: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "no_ci_claim_execution_until_native_frontier_clears"),
  decided: "2026-11-15",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-15, relayed verbatim by session neat-badger-614 (msg_e82068ff). Question as put to the operator, answering escalation msg_b70677a1: \"do you approve keeping claim execution out of gunbc CI until the native frontier clears?\". Operator words, verbatim: \"yes, i approved that already\". This is the EXPLICIT operator sign-off the CI contract requires; NOT the default-on-timeout posture -- the earlier msg_b70677a1 record was a recommended-option adopted BY DEFAULT ON TIMEOUT, which gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility forbids writing into this ledger (the home of dated OPERATOR rulings), so that pending-escalation stance is superseded here by the operator's explicit yes. WHAT IT GOVERNS: no CI claim-executing step is added -- neither a native instrument row nor a v1-interpreter claim_batch/floor stopgap; the required-witnesses-floor job stays deleted; no CI job runs witness claims today; a green v2-bootstrap is parse + typecheck only (nothing in CI executes claims -- gunbc.design_document::ci_contract_blocks). RESTORATION TRIGGER, named per gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder meta-obligation 3 (the trigger names the CAPABILITY restored): native claim row green on a required job -- gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn's own full restoration trigger (1a), the native binary executing the COMPLETE local_repo_wet_schedule claims on a required job with their host effects bounded, as a row judged by a binary built from the seed's emission, NEVER as a lane handing the corpus to the v1 interpreter (DESIGN, Reference: building and checks). A PARTIAL native row \"judges part of the population ... retires nothing\" (the drop's own words), so a step running only some members does not discharge this ruling.",
}

data codex_app_server_trip_pinned_oracle_repointed_to_typed_refusal: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "codex_app_server_trip_pinned_oracle_repointed_to_typed_refusal"),
  decided: "2026-11-15",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "guarantee_ladder"),
  provenance: "Operator ruling on PR gunbc#16536 (DP-M2 slice 2, row shell-dag-codex-app-server-trip-script), escalation msg_aa1d82e8, decided 2026-11-15, explicit and not a timeout default, directed to session gentle-ram-388. Asked to choose between re-pointing the pinned oracle arm test.claim.shell.shell_dag_codex_app_server_trip_script_witness.adversarial_codex_home_renders_no_shell_control_word to assert the typed CodexTripArgvRefused returned by gunbc.codex_app_server_press codex_press_account_trip_argv_admission, or leaving that row enrolled as expected-red, the operator answered, verbatim: \"Re-point pinned oracle to assert CodexTripArgvRefused.\" Endorsed by the witness owner session gentle-boar-614 (msg 902a0231), who withdrew his earlier hold on the re-point. The authorization covers THIS re-point only; the general do-not-hand-edit-this-pinned-oracle rule still governs any other change."
}

data pair_delivery_shell_as_medium_steps_admitted: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "pair_delivery_shell_as_medium_steps_admitted"),
  decided: "2026-11-18",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "fail_closed"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-18, to session neat-badger-614, answering 'Do you approve the restart and verify steps in #16623 running as shell scripts, as declared scaffolding, until each one's removal condition is met?': \"yes the two shell steps are okay\". RULED, in those words. This single ruling covers BOTH declared-scaffold legs on the release-pair delivery path in gunbc.live_deploy.release_pair_deliver_cli: (1) release_pair_verify_shell_scaffold / release_pair_verify_shell_dissolution_trigger -- the fleet consumer key-check verify runs as a multi-statement posix_sh program via posix_sh_program_command; DISSOLVES WHEN the consumer key-check is modeled as Bash grammar Nodes emitted through v2.workflow.bash_emit bash_emit_program (or a typed host-effect realization). (2) release_pair_restart_shell_scaffold / release_pair_restart_shell_dissolution_trigger -- the serve restart renders the single registered gunbc.live_deploy.operations::systemctl_restart_command (gunbc.auth.privileged_effect_registry:1376) to a String and posix_sh_program_command's it; DISSOLVES WHEN that authority returns an ArgvCommand under its existing privileged_effect_registry row. INTERPRETATION (mine, not the operator's words): the operator approved these two shell-as-medium STEPS as declared scaffolding only; it approves no NEW shell-as-medium leg, each leg still carries its own dissolution trigger, and each must be typed the moment its trigger is met. No second sudo restart builder is introduced."
}

data v2_landing_waits_on_the_namespace_program: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "v2_landing_waits_on_the_namespace_program"),
  decided: "2026-11-16",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "self_hosting"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-16, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), answering open question 1 of gunbc.plans.v2_self_host (is the namespace cleanup a prerequisite of v2 or its own program). Operator words, verbatim: \"regarding namespace cleanup - i'm not sure exactly - i always aim to separate concerns, but my concern is that, v2 should not be landing if the namespace cleanup is not finished, meaning, that seems to be a major blocker, because i don't even want v2 to support the import concept - i worry that we're straying quite far from that work.\" RULED, in those words: v2 does not land as the production route while the namespace program is unfinished. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the roadmap edge native-production-admission <- namespace-unique-on-chain is declared, so the namespace program gates production admission (D10) and nothing in native-seven, which keeps its 2026-11-08 scope (gunbc.design_ruling::n7_requires_only_its_own_closure, whose words separate the whole-repository program from N7 explicitly). NOT RULED HERE, AWAITING THE OPERATOR'S EXPLICIT WORD: the clause \"i don't even want v2 to support the import concept\" conflicts with gunbc.design_ruling::imports_are_the_terminal_name_mechanism (2026-11-08, D2: imports stay as the binding mechanism, import-grammar deletion withdrawn). A later statement does not silently supersede a recorded ruling (gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility); until the operator says which stands, D2 stands and namespace-unique-on-chain's handback is unchanged."
}

data native_frontier_step_budget_may_rise_once_its_checks_are_stated: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "native_frontier_step_budget_may_rise_once_its_checks_are_stated"),
  decided: "2026-11-16",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-16, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), answering open question 2 of gunbc.plans.v2_self_host (the native-frontier-required-lane step adds about 35 minutes per landing, and gunbc.design_ruling::ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth bans a time-budget increase without the operator's explicit sign-off). Operator words, verbatim: \"it can go up, i just need to know why/what the checks actually consist of now\". RULED, in those words: the required-job time budget may rise for the native-frontier step, conditional on the operator being told what the step checks. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the condition is discharged by gunbc.plans.v2_self_host section 7, which states what the required job runs today and what the step adds, read from gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate, gunbc.instrument_targets and v2.workflow.native_frontier_ratchet; the sign-off covers the step as the node describes it (a merge-queue-only step inside an existing lane, no new job, about 35 minutes, with the node's revisit trigger) and no other budget. gunbc.design_ruling::ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth otherwise stands."
}

data seed_deletion_observation_is_the_v1_programs_acceptance_gate: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "seed_deletion_observation_is_the_v1_programs_acceptance_gate"),
  decided: "2026-11-16",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-16, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), answering open question 4 of gunbc.plans.v2_self_host (SelfHostSeedRetirement reads NotDerivable until an instrument observes the seed families' absence; which node owns that instrument). Operator words, verbatim: \"i think this is just an acceptance gate on the project - like towards the end we need a clear project that confirms/winds down the rest of v1\". RULED, in those words: the seed-deletion observation is the acceptance gate of the v1 deletion program, not a separate instrument node. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the observation is owed by the terminal node of v1-deletion-program, v1-products-v1-free, whose handback already names the builds, the product runs and the list of anything deliberately kept; SelfHostSeedRetirement is read from that node's acceptance evidence, and the program's acceptance confirms and winds down the rest of v1. The same words answer the 2026-11-16 planning question of how the end of v1 is confirmed: by one clear project's acceptance, recorded as an event."
}

data imports_are_deleted_before_v2_is_accepted: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "imports_are_deleted_before_v2_is_accepted"),
  decided: "2026-11-16",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-16, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), closing the clause that gunbc.design_ruling::v2_landing_waits_on_the_namespace_program held open against gunbc.design_ruling::imports_are_the_terminal_name_mechanism. Operator words, verbatim: \"thanks, for keeping me consistent on the import stuff - i think my contradiction is basically that - i need to keep v2 moving, but also by the time v2 accepts, we should have killed imports for good - they cause a lot of problems - and i honestly feel that project has been consistently mishandled (by me mostly)\". RULED, in those words: deleting the import concept is restored as a goal, and it is sequenced: it must be done by the time v2 is accepted as the production route, and it does not stop v2 moving before then. SUPERSEDES the withdrawal half of the 2026-11-08 D2 ruling (import-grammar deletion withdrawn as a goal); D2's binding rule (a name binds by declaration, explicit import or qualified path) stands until the cut lands, and the 2026-11-08 D1 ruling (native-seven depends only on its own closure) stands in full. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the intention is gunbc.plans.names_without_imports; the roadmap edge native-production-admission <- namespace-unique-on-chain already declared under v2_landing_waits_on_the_namespace_program is the only ordering fixed; the interlock milestone NamespaceGrammarRetired, superseded 2026-11-08, is to be re-admitted as a prerequisite on its carrier by the realization that picks the program up, not by the plan change recording this ruling; the roadmap texts that say XL-7 is withdrawn carry a dated note rather than a rewrite."
}

data production_namespace_admission_precedes_seed_mirror_removal: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "production_namespace_admission_precedes_seed_mirror_removal"),
  decided: "2026-11-19",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "The dashboard approved the recommended option by default on 2026-11-19: \"Sole active resolver now, remove unused mirror with interpreter\" (decision msg_a9045090-303e-41a8-b39e-784034fbfc57 on msg_f2d4d9fd-86da-4a35-970b-255de401eec0). Production admission consumes evidence that the qualified resolver alone decides production declaration references and that both parsers reject imports. The old interpreter can still contain an unused bootstrap mirror. Its physical removal is verified with interpreter retirement."
}

data no_ratchets_names_bind_now_and_meaning_migrates_later: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "no_ratchets_names_bind_now_and_meaning_migrates_later"),
  decided: "2026-11-16",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "how_to_work"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-16, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), on the namespace migration (gunbc.plans.names_without_imports). Operator words, verbatim: \"We don't do ratchets anymore since they don't work well in this repo\"; \"I'd be more excited about a simpler grep that we can use with our own judgement that we try and falsify a few times\"; on deleting import lines and binding bare names to the one known declaration first, with the semantic migration as a separate step: \"yes, i'm fine with the idea of -> resolving to the actual known site now, and then handling the actual 'migration' as a separate step\"; and on priorities: \"i'm less worried about CI, than, doing a good enough job and making sure this migration finishes soon and correctly (while also note breaking everyones code)\". RULED, in those words: no new ratchet (a committed roster of permitted debt that may only shrink) is built for this program; the instrument is a simple, falsifiable reading used with judgment; the migration is two steps, names binding to their one known declaration first and meaning changes second; correctness and finishing soon rank above CI machinery. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the recommendation in gunbc.plans.names_without_imports and gunbc.plans.v2_self_host to enrol the native-frontier ratchet first is withdrawn; native-frontier-required-lane, which is a ratchet by its own name and carrier (v2.workflow.native_frontier_ratchet over native_frontier_roster), is to be re-read by its owner as a plain refusal over a closed population or retired, and the plan records that as open; the 2026-11-16 budget ruling for its step stands only for whatever that re-reading lands. The first reading is the bare-name grep recorded in the namespace plan's observation of the same date."
}

data declaration_references_are_fully_qualified: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "declaration_references_are_fully_qualified"),
  decided: "2026-11-17",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-17, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), answering the program review's layer-0 finding (a bare name bound by repository-wide spelling uniqueness is an ambient namespace, which the 2026-10-15 ruling forbids). Operator words, verbatim: \"regarding the namespace thing - actually, i was thinking of reversing an older decision of mine - can we just require fully qualified namespaces all the time for now? i think my preference was more for humans writing the code - we can probably add sugar on top of that later if needed? My concern is that making that distinction non trivial forks the design and creates a lot of ambiguity\". RULED, in those words: every reference to a declaration is spelled by its fully qualified path, all the time, for now; any sugar is a later addition on top of that rule. SUPERSEDES: the import arm and the bare-declaration arm of the 2026-10-15 cutover ruling (1) (a name reached by lexical scope, an explicit import or a qualified path) and the binding rule of gunbc.design_ruling::imports_are_the_terminal_name_mechanism; gunbc.design_ruling::imports_are_deleted_before_v2_is_accepted stands and is now realizable without a candidate-population rule. INTERPRETATION (not operator words, from the operator's stated concern that a non-trivial distinction forks the design): the rule has no exception for a module's own declarations, so a bare name is a lexical binder (a parameter, a let, a match or lambda binder, a type parameter) and nothing else, and a bare head that no binder answers is unbound and refuses; there is no import grammar, no import list, no ambient table and no candidate search; a module's dependency closure is the set of modules its qualified references name, read syntactically before any resolution; the v1 seed loads by that same reading (2026-10-15 rule 2: v1 mirrors v2), which is the one seed change the cut needs. The program is gunbc.plans.names_without_imports; its layers are re-cut under this rule in the same change."
}

data source_is_ingested_into_one_universe_and_files_are_discarded: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "source_is_ingested_into_one_universe_and_files_are_discarded"),
  decided: "2026-11-17",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "closed_grounded_substrate"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-17, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), on the compilation universe the program review and the architectural audit both named as missing. Operator words, verbatim: \"universe - I agree this is a missing concept - basically what i imagined int he beginning was something like this - you send a bundle of files and we immediatley parse/discard the actual files - we can keep a mapping, but i want to get away from file mappings as quickly as possible - this would mean a large set of files is passed in as a universe/separate step - so most of the compiler doesn't have to care/know much about it (except for literal text/file stuff) - my inspiration for that is to separate the code from the actual files as much as possible - the vision is a file-less system where things are purely named, no more files at all. I think we would have a giant code blob basically (for now, a single universe) - but a fractured universe is plausible over time\". RULED, in those words: source enters as a bundle at one ingest step that parses it into a universe of declarations and discards the files; a mapping from declaration to its source text and positions is kept for literal text and location only; everything after ingest is purely named; one universe for now, fracturing admitted later. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the universe is the compilation subject the audit asked for, with one global namespace in which two providers for one path refuse and every declaration is public until a visibility model has a consumer; there is no file-level semantic unit, so no module or namespace header, and each declaration spells its own full path (a header may return as sugar once the core is stable); a test declaration is an ordinary declaration whose selection is a property of the subject, not a second universe; the closure of a subject is the declaration-demand fixed point over authored and compiler-generated demands (std.generated_provider), and reading a file is the ingest step's realization, never a semantic fact. gunbc.plans.names_without_imports and gunbc.plans.v2_self_host carry this."
}

data phase_admission_is_a_conformance_pattern: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "phase_admission_is_a_conformance_pattern"),
  decided: "2026-11-17",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "conformance"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-17, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), on the audit's sealed compiler phases. Operator words, verbatim: \"What is a phase? is that like a state machine? if its like a common pattern int he repo, we can add it, basically it sounds like a conformance pattern we're missing.\" MEASURED (not operator words): the pattern exists and is common: a phase is a carrier type whose only constructor is the function that establishes it (the language's sole_constructor modifier, 2,041 uses across the tree), so that a later phase cannot be built from the earlier phase's raw representation; in the compiler it is v2.compiler.normalized_tree NormalizedTree and CensusTree, v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold BodyLoweredTree and v2.compiler.module_acquisition HeldSourceRead, while ParseTree is still a bare alias of Node and no CheckedProgram exists. RULED, in those words: phase admission is a conformance domain, added to gunbc.design_argument conformance_domains in the same change; the operator's reading of it as a state machine is exact, with the states as types and the transitions as the only constructors. INTERPRETATION: v2's vision milestone (gunbc.plans.v2_self_host) requires every phase sealed, four phases rather than eight (parsed, resolved, checked, emitted), and evaluation and emission consuming only the checked carrier, which is what replaces a roster of tolerated unknowns."
}

data operations_are_declarations_with_realizations: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "operations_are_declarations_with_realizations"),
  decided: "2026-11-17",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "closed_grounded_substrate"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-17, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), on the audit's canonical operations. Operator words, verbatim: \"regarding operations - i was hoping that these are not meaningfully different and more like a sugar concept over a function/one of our primtives basically - i would trace what they are today so we can see\". TRACED (not operator words): the substrate's primitives are five connectives (Atom, Conj, Disj, Arrow, Instantiation) and six behaviors (Value, Transform, Branch, Loop, Bind, Match) in v2.std.node, and nothing else is primitive; every operation the corpus uses is, or can be, an ordinary declaration with a realization row per target, as std.algebra fold_list already is (a declared fn whose host realization is a HostRealizedSeam row). Five mechanisms today carry that one shape in five spellings: 68 std.primitives contracts, 120 builtin_registry_surface_names, 40 primitive_projection rows, the CanonicalOperation enum that surface operators lower to and that infer, target_serialize and three target models consume, and the seed's 134 v1_rt functions. RULED, in those words: an operation is not a concept distinct from a function; it is a declaration with a realization, and an operator or collection form is sugar that lowers to a reference to that declaration. INTERPRETATION: the five mechanisms converge on declaration plus realization row; CanonicalOperation becomes a set of declarations (the operand's algebra: add, compare, coerce) with per-target rows, and the lowering of `+` is a reference to one of them; this is folded into gunbc.plans.names_without_imports layer 0 and gunbc.plans.v2_self_host's vision milestone, not staffed before the qualified-reference cut."
}

data effort_estimates_are_public_and_live_in_gunbc: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "effort_estimates_are_public_and_live_in_gunbc"),
  decided: "2026-11-17",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-17, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), asked where milestone estimates should be recorded. Operator words, verbatim: \"i think public planning is fine - sorry, i should probably reverse that - i think the private/public split should be for anything that we actually care to be private - i don't care if people see our effort esimtates etc\". RULED, in those words: planning, effort estimates included, is public and lives in this repository; the private repository holds only what is actually private. SUPERSEDES, in part, gunbc.design_ruling::planning_scheduling_is_cut: a dated effort estimate with a basis is not the schedule, forecast or time-period record that ruling cut; it is the clock the operator asked to keep on 2026-11-03 in the private plan (\"the clock is the estimate, not the milestone\": every estimate dated, saying when the work starts and finishes and why, appended and never edited, so the first estimate and the actual completion stay readable side by side). INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the home is gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_effort_estimate, one row per estimate keyed by the roadmap node that stands for the milestone; an estimate names its basis and is appended, never edited; overdue is derived from the latest estimate against the acceptance log, never authored; the private plan's rows point here for the public milestones rather than restating them (gunbc.design_ruling::planning_is_one_source_for_every_repo); no date ever becomes a dependency or a gate."
}

data claims_name_their_leaf_from_the_first_landing: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "claims_name_their_leaf_from_the_first_landing"),
  decided: "2026-11-18",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-18, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), asked whether a Claimed event that names only its work item (no leaf path from gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_decomposition) is admitted on a multi-leaf item during the migration to leaf-grain claims, or refused from the first landing. Operator words, verbatim: \"its onlya dmitted if its an exponential amount of work to fit the repo - prefer to not and track anything outstanding and prioritize it\". RULED, in those words: a claim names its leaf from the first landing; a pathless claim is refused at append unless fitting the repository would be an exponential amount of work, and anything left outstanding by the refusal is tracked and prioritized, not admitted. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): the migration arm of unit 4 of the dispatch brief is not built; a live item-level claim that predates the landing is re-recorded against its leaf or released, and the set of such claims is a work item, not an admitted shape."
}

data a_split_handback_is_accepted_by_the_manager: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "a_split_handback_is_accepted_by_the_manager"),
  decided: "2026-11-18",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-18, direct to session eloquent-albattani (gunb-ai/gunbc#16604), asked who accepts a Split handback (a worker's replacement decomposition for the leaf it held, which edits gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_decomposition and can move the critical path). Operator words, verbatim: \"yeah the manager canown it\". RULED, in those words: the manager of the owning session accepts a Split, as it accepts any work item (gunbc.design_ruling::work_item_acceptance_is_the_managers_or_an_escalation). INTERPRETATION (not operator words): no operator gate on splits that lengthen the critical path; the view flags a split that moved the critical path so the operator sees it rather than approves it."
}

// ESTIMATES ARE ONE REPRESENTATION, DERIVED UPWARD (operator, 2026-11-19). The no-dual-representations
// rule applied to planning: a node's estimate is named ONCE, in ONE of the two forms the author chooses,
// and every other number about it -- the other form, its dates, its parent's total -- is derived from that one.
data estimates_are_one_representation_derived_upward: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "estimates_are_one_representation_derived_upward"),
  decided: "2026-11-19",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-19, to session neat-badger-614, on whether a roadmap node may carry a T-shirt size and a duration at once. Operator words, verbatim, first: \"i think t shirt size is fine if it covers your use case - i'm not sure i quite get the intention right now - i think what we could do is have a one way derivation like, you could specify a t shirt size OR a literal estimate, your choice -a nd we could derive the other consistently\"; then, asked what happens when two estimates disagree: \"yeah i think my point is, basically the estimates can't contradict each other - if a project node says 1w, but a single subnode says 2w, that is a contradiction - so we should derive as much as possible (basically just the no dual representations applied to this planning)\"; and, asked whether to proceed: \"Yeah you can go ahead\".  RULED, IN THOSE WORDS: an estimate names EITHER a T-shirt size OR a literal duration, at the author's choice, and the other is derived from it consistently; estimates cannot contradict each other; derive as much as possible -- the no-dual-representations rule applied to planning.  WHAT IS INTERPRETATION, NOT OPERATOR WORDS, correctable by the operator: (1) THE ONE REPRESENTATION is one row naming either a size or a duration in WORKING DAYS, plus the date it was made and its basis; it is appended and never edited, the discipline gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_effort_estimate already keeps. Hours appear nowhere. (2) ONE CONVERSION TABLE is the single authority between the two forms: each size names a working-day range, the ranges partition the positive whole numbers so every duration falls in exactly one size, size to duration takes that range's midpoint rounded up, and duration to size is the bucket containing it. Its basis is the measured data in gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_effort_estimate's header (an easy node is about a week; a hard compiler node 10 to 16 weeks, the same 50-to-80-working-day span the first native-seven estimate itself carries) and the trigger that re-calibrates it is REALIZED durations: gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_leaf_realized_duration. (3) DERIVED UPWARD means a node with any estimated descendant -- a descendant along the hierarchy gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_model calls the primary child, or a decomposition piece where one exists -- has its estimate COMPUTED there and never authored: total work is the sum over its children, elapsed time the critical path over the edges between them under a declared worker count. An authored estimate on such a node IS the contradiction, refused by a typed arm naming both sides; the operator's own case, a project at 1w beside one subnode at 2w, is the red control. (4) Unsized is a LISTED state, never a price: no node is defaulted to a size, to a band's floor, or to zero.  WHAT IT NARROWS, WHAT IT INTERPRETS, WHAT STILL HOLDS: NARROWS the note \"SIZE IS DERIVED, NEVER AUTHORED\" at the head of gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_sizing -- that note is a module design note, not an operator ruling, and the words above admit a size a person states. A stated size is admissible only as a LABELLED ESTIMATE WITH A BASIS, never as a measured fact; what the note was written against, an unlabelled size read as evidence, stays refused. That module's SizingSubject/Intricacy/Volume pair is untouched: it is a derivation input about a task, not an estimate of a node. INTERPRETS gunbc.design_ruling::effort_estimates_are_public_and_live_in_gunbc, whose rows author start, done and pessimistic DATES: under no-dual-representations those dates are DERIVED -- from the duration, the dependency edges and the worker count, bound to dates at the read -- and are never authored beside a duration; the row keeps its made date, its basis and its append-only history, which is the clock that ruling asked to keep. CLOSES the dated carrier to new rows: the rows already appended to gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_effort_estimate are history, none is deleted or rewritten, the check reports on them as they stand naming both sides of every conflict, and the carrier takes no new row -- a new row would author a date beside a duration. planning_scheduling_is_cut STILL HOLDS: no date and no estimate is ever a dependency or a gate, and what that ruling cut -- a stored schedule, an allocation the operator does not consume -- stays cut. schedule_answers_are_computed_cached_and_re_read STILL HOLDS: the item-level Gantt over the whole roadmap is computed at the read from these carriers, and a cache of it is derived and never authoritative."
}

data schedule_answers_are_computed_cached_and_re_read: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "schedule_answers_are_computed_cached_and_re_read"),
  decided: "2026-11-18",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "Operator, 2026-11-18, answering whether a schedule answer computed at the read -- start and finish per leaf, the lanes, the critical path and the next-leaf offer -- is inside gunbc.design_ruling::planning_scheduling_is_cut, which cut scheduling on 2026-11-11. Operator words, verbatim: \"yeah we cut the schedulinga nd are redoing it now - but yes, you should compute it/cache it/re-read it - the planning we did now is relevant\". RULED, in those words: the schedule is COMPUTED from the carriers and the item event log; it MAY be cached; a cache is derived and NEVER authoritative, so no cached answer is a schedule type, a time-period record, forecast state or a synchronized today list; and the answer is RECOMPUTED ON READ. This narrows gunbc.design_ruling::planning_scheduling_is_cut, whose target was the stored schedule and the operator's own consumption of it, not an answer derived at the read; and it stands beside gunbc.design_ruling::effort_estimates_are_public_and_live_in_gunbc, which made a dated estimate with a basis the clock. INTERPRETATION (not operator words): \"the planning we did now is relevant\" affirms the brief this lane was given, whose done-when is that the next-leaf offer is accepted by the belt as its spawn input -- so the offer is the belt's selection basis among leaves the readiness predicate already admits, bounded by BeltCapacity, and the readiness predicate stays the gate on what may start at all. Correctable by the operator."
}

data roadmap_scheduling_units_belong_to_factory: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "roadmap_scheduling_units_belong_to_factory"),
  decided: "2026-11-19",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "plans_are_intentions"),
  provenance: "The operator chose \"Existing factory project\" for the containment of the 14 scheduling and dispatch units declared by gunb-ai/gunbc#16650 (decision msg_0b5d7e3d-f565-4acb-adca-e80d4d375669 on msg_47ac36f4-44c1-4a80-ac9f-daf987042dd1). The factory project owns the daily workspace and workers. The units serve gunbc.plans.planning_consolidation."
}

data the_ctrl_dashboard_renders_the_planning_page: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
