fn ruling_blocks(r: DesignRuling) -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: concat(r.reference.decl_name as String, " — ", r.decided, " — ", declaration_ref_display_key(ref: r.governs))),
    p(text: r.provenance),
  ]
}

fn expected_design_rulings_md() -> String {
  serialize_markdown_source(doc: MarkdownDocument {
    blocks: concat(
      [
        HeadingBlock { level: H1, inlines: [TextInline { text: "gunbc — design rulings" }] },
        p(text: "Generated on demand from `gunbc.design_ruling`. `DESIGN.md` states each rule and cites its row; a row carries only the date, the claim it governs and the provenance, never a second copy of the rule."),
        hr(),
      ],
      flat_map(design_rulings(), r => ruling_blocks(r: r)),
    ),
  })
}

// THE OPERATOR'S 2026-11-19 TRIM OF THE H11SSL-i UNIT 3 SERVING CEILING, WHICH IS WHAT UNBLOCKS THAT
// UNIT'S CI SEAT. The ruling changes what is DECIDED; the physical ceiling moves only when a read says it
// has, so the row below keeps the two apart rather than writing a number nobody has measured.
data ken_strata_memory_trim_buys_two_build_seats_2026_11_19: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "ken_strata_memory_trim_buys_two_build_seats_2026_11_19"),
  decided: "2026-11-19",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Relayed to me by smart-crane-730 from operator chat on 2026-11-19. The proposal was put to the operator by smart-crane-730: lower the ken-strata resident ceiling from 112G to 56G so the unit's declared CI seat stops being blocked by a serving charge larger than the serving need, while serving keeps full speed. The operator's answer, verbatim: \"yeah thats fine w/ me thanks\". The arithmetic the proposal was put on is also the operator's: MemTotal 125.6 GiB on that unit, less a 56 GiB serving reservation, leaves 69.6 GiB, which is two 25 GiB build seats plus roughly 20 GiB of OS headroom -- so the unit carries TWO CI slots at that ceiling.

THE SERVING-SIDE FIGURES ARE THE OPERATOR'S REPORT, NOT READS THIS FLEET HAS TAKEN. The operator performed a ken memory trim, live on the unit from 17:21Z on 2026-11-19: parked-session RAM 32 -> 12 GiB; parked-session slots 24 -> 6; parallel 8 -> 4; non-reclaimable memory roughly 59 -> roughly 25 GB; the full-speed need roughly 53 GB, which the operator splits as about 25 GB of engine plus about 28 GiB of model page cache; below that level prompt prefill degrades and THE DEGRADATION CURVE IS UNMEASURED; max context stays 224k; the prior configuration is kept on the host as cfg-ken.json.bak-before-mem-trim-20261119T171122Z.

WHAT THE MODEL DOES NOT YET KNOW, which is the part a reader must not skip. No read held in this repo has confirmed the new ceiling: gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory::ken_strata_engine_memory still carries memory_max 112 GiB, from the 2026-11-18T21:21Z systemctl read over read-only ssh, and its soft ceiling, swap ceiling, loaded-and-idle base and parked-session cache are still ServingMemoryUnread with the reads that would settle them named. The owed read is systemctl show ken-strata.service for MemoryMax, MemoryHigh and MemorySwapMax on that host, plus the parked-session cache budget and slot count from cfg-ken.json; only a read like that may move the physical row, because fail_closed (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed) refuses a plausible output nobody observed. Once it lands, the consequence this ruling was made for is mechanical rather than argued: gunbc.serving.serving_reservation derives the serving charge from the read ceiling, so the frontier row h11ssl_unit3_serving_charge_blocks_build_seat_frontier should dissolve, and that row -- not this ruling -- is where the two-seat claim lives until a read exists to put it there.",
}

// THE THREE OPERATOR DECISIONS THE BOOT AND MANAGEMENT EDGE INTENTION STANDS ON THAT WERE RECORDED
// NOWHERE BUT A PLAN CARRIER. DESIGN.md's *Plans are intentions* is explicit -- "a decision it needs
// from the operator is recorded in `gunbc.design_ruling` and cited, never kept in the plan or the pull
// request alone" -- so these three are recorded here and cited from
// gunbc.plans.new_server_bringup, which no longer holds them as prose. Their only source is that
// carrier -- section 1 and section 7 of the file on branch session/smart-crane-730-plan (head
// c623b01ef18), which smart-crane-730 relayed from operator chat on 2026-11-10 and never opened a pull
// request for, so until this change no row in the repository carried them. NO VERBATIM OPERATOR MESSAGE
// SURVIVES for these three: each row quotes the carrier's decision text and says so, and any framing that
// is mine rather than the operator's is marked INTERPRETATION inside the provenance, so a reader can tell
// the relayed words from the reading of them (manager ruling msg_98ba7c8a, 2026-11-20). Where a decision is
// already modeled, the row below cites the model instead of restating it, and no ruling is minted for it.
data pxe_default_install_and_no_standing_erase_2026_11_10: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "pxe_default_install_and_no_standing_erase_2026_11_10"),
  decided: "2026-11-10",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.site_pxe_edge_converge", decl_name: "site_pxe_edge_no_install_requests"),
  provenance: "Decides the default installation path and the lifetime of an install request. SOURCE OF THE WORDS: relayed by smart-crane-730 in gunbc.plans.new_server_bringup on branch session/smart-crane-730-plan at c623b01ef18, section 1 (\"1. Operator decisions (2026-11-10)\"), relayed from chat on 2026-11-10. That branch never had a pull request and its head is not on main, so it is cited here as the branch file the words were taken from, never as a landed change; the repository holds no other record of the exchange, so what is quoted below is quoted from that file, not from an operator message that survives in the repository. The decision text, verbatim from that file: \"PXE is the default installation path for every eligible physical machine. BMC virtual media remains a fallback that needs an explicit, evidenced reason.\" and \"An install is one-shot. An install request is never a standing authorization to erase a disk.\" INTERPRETATION (mine, 2026-11-20, not the operator's words): these are two halves of one decision -- a default plus a one-shot exception to it -- and both are already modeled rather than restated here: the eligible architectures are uefi_pxe_admitted_architectures at gunbc.network_boot_delivery:221, and gunbc.fleet.site_pxe_edge_converge::site_pxe_edge_no_install_requests is the roster of standing install requests, kept empty, with each install a client binding carrying its own target-disk identity. The ruling changes what is decided; the empty roster is what refuses a standing erase.",
}

data runner_capacity_counts_only_when_observed_2026_11_10: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "runner_capacity_counts_only_when_observed_2026_11_10"),
  decided: "2026-11-10",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "fail_closed"),
  provenance: "Decides when a machine counts as runner capacity. SOURCE OF THE WORDS: relayed by smart-crane-730 in gunbc.plans.new_server_bringup on branch session/smart-crane-730-plan at c623b01ef18, section 1 (\"1. Operator decisions (2026-11-10)\"), relayed from chat on 2026-11-10. That branch never had a pull request and its head is not on main, so it is cited here as the branch file the words were taken from, never as a landed change; the repository holds no other record of the exchange, so what is quoted below is quoted from that file, not from an operator message that survives in the repository. The decision text, verbatim from that file: \"Runner capacity is a completion condition. A machine counts as capacity only after its runner services are healthy, its registrations and labels are observed, its resource admission resolves, and a real CI job has run on it.\" INTERPRETATION (mine, 2026-11-20, not the operator's words): a host that exists is not a host that contributes, so a fleet view counting the first would report capacity nobody observed -- fail_closed (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed) applied to a capacity number. Where the observations come from: gunbc.runner.runner_host_deploy (gunbc_runner_listener_principal at :218, srv1_runner_host_spec at :220), where srv14/butch is the already-installed CI-only host at :341 and a spec refuses until its host's LAN address is modeled.",
}

data pxe_witnesses_need_no_required_ci_2026_11_10: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "pxe_witnesses_need_no_required_ci_2026_11_10"),
  decided: "2026-11-10",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "conformance"),
  provenance: "Decides that the PXE witnesses do not join required CI. SOURCE OF THE WORDS: relayed by smart-crane-730 in gunbc.plans.new_server_bringup on branch session/smart-crane-730-plan at c623b01ef18, section 7 (\"7. Decided: no required CI for the PXE witnesses\"), relayed from chat on 2026-11-10. That branch never had a pull request and its head is not on main, so it is cited here as the branch file the words were taken from, never as a landed change; the repository holds no other record of the exchange, so what is quoted below is quoted from that file, not from an operator message that survives in the repository. The carrier's sentence, verbatim from that file: \"The operator ruled on 2026-11-10 that the PXE witnesses do not join required CI now. Nothing on the required path evaluates a witness (the declared drop gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn), and the job roster and budgets stay closed to growth (gunbc.design_ruling job_roster_closed_to_growth, ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth). Each lane runs the witnesses it touches with claim_batch and records the result in its PR.\" Its opening clause is the decision; the rest is smart-crane-730's gloss of the model, so it is cited and not repeated: that drop and those two rulings are rows of gunbc.rung_drop and gunbc.design_ruling. INTERPRETATION (mine, 2026-11-20, not the operator's words): this narrows conformance (gunbc.design_argument::conformance) for this class of witness only, because a check needing a machine on the network and a hand at a console would block every unrelated change whenever the floor is busy -- so its verdict is carried by the work item's own acceptance, not branch protection. It does not weaken the witness: the claim still goes RED against a plant and GREEN against the fix, on the post-wall binary, before the item that owes it is accepted.",
}


data heal_convergence_job_budget_increase_2026_11_20: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "heal_convergence_job_budget_increase_2026_11_20"),
  decided: "2026-11-20",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_document", decl_name: "ci_contract_blocks"),
  provenance: "Operator, directly in session merry-moth-343 on 2026-11-20, after the diagnosis of heal run 44676645073 on gunbc#16761: \"yeah could you give this more budget/variable budget now? and then could you give me the cleanup/performance stuff after\". This is an explicit instruction to increase this heal job budget, not a timeout-default escalation. IMPLEMENTATION CHOICE, not a numeric quotation from the operator: increase gunbc.ci.repository_ci_profile::gunbc_repository_ci_profile heal.timeout from 90 to 120 minutes; the assistant stated that choice in its next response. The measured run completed its third generation with first_generation_equal=true at 06:17:03Z and was cancelled at 06:17:08Z by the 90-minute job backstop before repair publication. The extra 30 minutes allows publication and runtime variation. Scope: only the heal-generated-artifacts job; no witness timeout, memory envelope, generation bound or check is relaxed. The operator also requested the cleanup/performance follow-up, so this allowance does not discharge the repeated regeneration and scope-selection work tracked by gunbc.plans.read_set_convergence.",
}


data text_of_chars_is_a_whole_list_host_realization: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "text_of_chars_is_a_whole_list_host_realization"),
  decided: "2026-11-20",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "closed_grounded_substrate"),
  provenance: "Operator decision msg_65796359-9ec0-4dec-b4a3-b2e29621206a: revise the 2026-10-25 NOT A SEAM decision and model std.host_text::text_of_chars as a declaration-identity host realization. The existing operation is the total whole-list inverse of chars over Unicode scalar values, realized by a linear host writer. This neither declares nor equates the arbitrary-window chars_to_string operation: its clamping and invalid-Int behavior remain separate. Preserve round trips and linear cost; no copied-accumulator .dag substitute and no ambient-name resolver exception.",
}


data claim_execution_has_one_v1_entrypoint: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "claim_execution_has_one_v1_entrypoint"),
  decided: "2026-11-22",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "single_authority"),
  provenance: "Operator, directly in session lively-dove-824 on 2026-11-22. After an explanation of what claim_batch does and how it differs from gunbc test, the operator asked, verbatim: \"basically i'm wondering if we can consolidate/simplify this so that there is onlya  single, optimal, correct process? i don't like having both claim batch and gunbc test\". Offered two options -- (A) consolidate in v1 now: gunbc test <target-pattern> becomes the only claim entrypoint, the claim_batch binary and gunbc run --claim-run are deleted, and one verdict rule (gunbc.target_invocation) is shared; or (B) only unify the verdict rule now and delete the extra entrypoints when the native route judges claims -- the operator answered, verbatim: \"Yeah could you please plan/design this and come back with something? I'd prefer A\". WHAT IT ADMITS: work on the semantics-frozen seed (gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing::v1_seed_standing) that deletes claim_batch and gunbc run --claim-run, repairs the seed adapter of the claim route's existing verdict rule, and carries onto gunbc test only what an executing caller needs; the repeated operands and the execution-mode operand that adds are PublicSurfaceGrowth admitted only inside the same change that deletes the two surfaces they replace. It does not admit deleting a route a caller still needs: a caller whose observation cannot yet be preserved keeps its route, frozen, until native support lands. WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE: gunbc.design_ruling::no_ci_claim_execution_until_native_frontier_clears still holds, so no CI claim-executing step is added. The caller contract is meant to survive the seed: the native compiler later implements the same interface and result rule. The intention is gunbc.plans.claim_execution_consolidation.",
}


// The authority for dissolving the known-red transport decoder, recorded where DESIGN.md puts an operator
// decision (gunbc.design_ruling) rather than in the carrier it dissolves: a consent that dies with the carrier
// would vanish exactly when it is used, and a comment is not a record. Read the wording precisely: an approval
// that arrived by default is stated as that, not promoted into a sign-off it never was.
data known_red_decoder_dissolution_approved_by_default_2026_11_22: DesignRuling = DesignRuling {
  reference: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "known_red_decoder_dissolution_approved_by_default_2026_11_22"),
  decided: "2026-11-22",
  governs: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "epistemic_humility"),
  provenance: "Approved by default, not signed. The escalation asking to dissolve the known-red transport decoder (msg_72943fe5) closed 2026-11-22 with no operator answer within 15 minutes, and the recommended option, dissolve now, stood approved by default. That is what it is and what it is not: it is not an operator sign-off, and it is not the harness lane's consent. The operator's 2026-10-21 admission of the known-red transport decoder scaffold carried three conditions, one of them the harness lane's consent, and that consent was never obtained: sunny-bear-433 was unreachable (recipient session not found) and did not sign. What stands is the dissolution: the instrument decodes each per-claim line of the single v1 claim entrypoint into v2.workflow.floor_terminal_ledger ClaimAttemptTerminal by a total mapping over the closed outcome kinds, an unknown kind refusing with exit 2 rather than falling into a default arm; the prose marker decoder, its predicates and its extractors are deleted; and the decoder's declaration ref is retargeted off v1_compiler.claim_batch::claim_run_outcomes onto the decoder that replaces it. It governs the 2026-10-21 admission, whose carrier is the deferral row in gunbc.instruments.known_red_derivation. It executes in the claim-entrypoints-cutover change (gunb-ai/gunbc#16872) once claim-result-contract (gunb-ai/gunbc#16873) is accepted, because the line it decodes is that item's schema."
}
